	<!-- Export compliance (US EAR Category 5 Part 2). Declared here so a TestFlight upload is
	     never parked at "Missing Compliance" waiting on a manual answer in App Store Connect.
	     The release lane does not wait for build processing, so it has no opportunity to answer
	     that question after the fact - see ios-swiftUI/fastlane/Fastfile.

	     <false/> asserts that T'Day contains no NON-EXEMPT encryption. What it actually uses:
	       - AES-256-GCM (CryptoKit) in Tday/Core/Data/Auth/CredentialEnvelope.swift, wrapping
	         the sign-in username/password for transport to the user's own server;
	       - RSA-2048 OAEP-SHA256 (Security) in the same file, wrapping that AES key;
	       - AES-256-GCM (CryptoKit) in Tday/Core/Security/ProbeDecryptor.swift, decrypting the
	         server's version-compatibility probe - note this one is confidentiality applied to
	         ordinary application data (appVersion / updateRequired / compatibilityMode) using a
	         key bundled in the app, NOT authentication;
	       - SHA-256 (CryptoKit) in NetworkConfiguration/TodayTasksWidget - hashing, not
	         encryption;
	       - HTTPS/TLS via URLSession.

	     THE BASIS FOR THE EXEMPTION, in order of how cleanly it fits:

	       1. Encryption limited to that provided within the operating system. Every primitive
	          above is Apple's own CryptoKit / Security / URLSession. T'Day implements no cipher,
	          bundles no third-party crypto library, and ships no modified crypto. This one
	          covers ProbeDecryptor as well as the credential envelope.
	       2. Publicly available source code (EAR 740.13(e)). T'Day is open source at
	          github.com/ohmzi/Tday. NOTE: this exemption carries a ONE-TIME email notification
	          to BIS (crypt@bis.doc.gov) and the NSA (enc@nsa.gov) giving the source URL. If the
	          account owner wants to rely on it, that email has to be sent.

	     Deliberately NOT relied on:
	       - "limited to authentication" alone. It covers CredentialEnvelope but not
	         ProbeDecryptor, which decrypts application data rather than authenticating anything.
	         An earlier draft of this comment claimed it did; that was wrong.
	       - "short key length". AES-256 / RSA-2048 exceed the 56-bit symmetric / 512-bit
	         asymmetric thresholds, so this exemption does not apply.

	     THE ACCOUNT OWNER MUST CONFIRM THIS DECLARATION BEFORE THE FIRST UPLOAD - it is a legal
	     export statement, not a build setting. If they are not comfortable with the above, the
	     alternative is to set this key to <true/> and file the annual self-classification
	     report with BIS; the pipeline still works, but the first build of each version then
	     needs the compliance question answered by hand in App Store Connect.
	     See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md - Export compliance. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
